{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>Slain by the sword or by the dead.</b> It should have said 'by the slain or by the dead'; the reason Scripture mentioned 'sword' is that it speaks of what is common. And when it says 'slain by the sword,' the same applies to one slain by stone or wood. Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'a sword is like the slain.' The reason is that the sword is susceptible to impurity, and it is an 'father of fathers of impurity' (avi avot ha-tum'ah) like the corpse itself when it touches a dead body, for it is the cause of destruction and from the side of the destructive force. That which has the status of 'slain' serves to defile a person and vessels with seven-day impurity, but not to defile through tent-impurity (tum'at ohel), since it is written (Numbers 19:14): ״אדם כי ימות באהל״ — 'a man when he dies in a tent' — a man defiles in a tent, but a sword does not. For if the sword that a person touched were to defile through tent-impurity, all the priests would be forbidden to enter houses, for in every one of them there is an impure sword and it would defile that house through tent-impurity — as in the matter that the Rav, R. Hayyim of blessed memory, sent from Ashkenaz to Rabbenu Tam of blessed memory: ״אי זה בית אשר תבנו לי״ ('What house could you build for Me?'). And what they said, 'a sword is like the slain,' should not be understood as referring exclusively to a sword, but the same applies to all types of metal: if they touched a corpse, their law is like the corpse itself. Nor should it be understood as referring to the sword with which the slain person was killed, according to the opinion of some of the Geonim of blessed memory; rather, certainly all other types of metal have the status of a sword, and all of them are like the corpse itself: if they touched an avi avot ha-tum'ah, they are avi avot ha-tum'ah, and if they touched an av ha-tum'ah, they are av ha-tum'ah. This is the principle: all types of metal have the law of the slain, for they are susceptible to impurity from that which they touch, being like it. And this is from what they said in tractate Shabbat, chapter HaZorek: Shmuel said, 'this is when it is tied with an iron chain.' And these words apply to impurity, for the Merciful One said ״בחלל חרב״ — 'a sword is like the slain.' And this makes clear that the sword and the iron chain are equivalent regarding impurity. And furthermore, in the first chapter of Pesachim: here we deal with a metal lamp, for the Merciful One said ״בחלל חרב, חרב הרי הוא כחלל״ — and this is clearly when the lamp touched a corpse, not that the corpse was killed by it. And we learned in the Mishnah: the fathers of impurity are the creeping thing, semen, and one defiled by a corpse. And since one who touches a corpse is an av ha-tum'ah, the corpse itself is avi avot ha-tum'ah. Now it is well known that impurity extends through six levels and no more, and these are the six levels of impurity: avi avot; its second is av ha-tum'ah; its third is rishon le-tum'ah; its fourth is sheni be-chulin; its fifth is shlishi be-terumah; its sixth is revi'i be-kodesh. We do not find impurity ascending beyond six, for purity cleaves to the seventh level, which is called 'pure waters' in the time of redemption, as it is written (Ezekiel 36:25): ״וזרקתי עליכם מים טהורים״, and in the time of exile it is called 'the gate of tears,' for tears come from the power of water. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: all the gates were locked except the gate of tears. And the seventh level is called by our Rabbis of blessed memory 'upper waters,' and it is the attribute of Hesed, which is seventh from below upward, and they are called 'living waters' — as the prophet said (Zechariah 14:8): ״ביום ההוא יצאו מים חיים מירושלים״ — and this is the Jerusalem above. And from this you will find in the account of the second day [of Creation] 'waters' five times, and on the third day 'waters' twice, and all are seven, for the work of the waters was not completed until the third day. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in tractate Makhshirin, chapter 8: there are seven liquids — water, wine, oil, honey, blood, dew, and milk. The first corresponds to water, which is the liquid, and the six correspond to the six derivatives within it. And each of these seven liquids, Scripture mentions in it the language of drinking or the language of giving drink: water, as it is written (Exodus 17:6) ״ויצא ממנו מים ושתה העם״ and it is written (Genesis 24:43) ״השקיני נא מעט מים״; wine, as it is written (Leviticus 10:9) ״יין ושכר אל תשת״ and it is written (Jeremiah 35:2) ״והשקית אותם יין״; oil, as it is written (Isaiah 25:6) ״משתה שמנים״; blood, as it is written (Numbers 23:24) ״ודם חללים ישתה״ and it is written (Ezekiel 39:17) ״ואכלתם בשר ושתיתם דם״; dew is called water, as it is written (Judges 6:38) ״וימץ טל מן הגזה מלא הספל מים״; honey, as it is written (Deuteronomy 32:13) ״ויניקהו דבש מסלע״; milk, as it is written (Judges 4:19) ״ותפתח את נאד החלב ותשקהו״."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The citation משתה שמנים is from Isaiah 25:6; the verse contains the term שמן (oil) but not a drinking verb directly, though the word משתה (banquet/drinking-feast) carries that sense. The citation for honey (Deuteronomy 32:13) contains ויניקהו (suckled/gave to suck) rather than a standard drinking or giving-drink verb, though the root ינק conveys nursing."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:34:15.279256167Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}